<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" elementFormDefault="qualified" attributeFormDefault="unqualified" version="2.0">
  <xs:annotation>
    <xs:documentation>
REQUEST schema for getPersonCompleteDetailsB (Customer360) -- rebuilt 2026-10-12 against the
loyalty-app service generation.

Transport
  POST /loyalty-app/control/getPersonCompleteDetailsB, form field "XmlInput" carrying this XML.
  Header "accessTokenKey" is validated before the search runs (failure: RESPONSE_CODE E899).
  Every outcome is reported inside the response document; see the response schema.

How the service reads this document
  - Only CRITERIA is read. Element names are matched without regard to case and in any order;
    elements the service does not recognise are ignored without error.
  - There is no CUSTOMER "anchor" form: a REQUEST without CRITERIA returns E105.
  - Values are used as sent. No format validation is applied to e-mail, phone, postal code or
    state values by this operation, so no patterns are enforced here.

At least one of these must be non-empty, otherwise E105:
  CUSTOMER_ID, SYSTEM_REFERENCE, SYSTEM_REFERENCE_ID, EMAIL_ID, PHONE_NUMBER, FIRST_NAME,
  LAST_NAME, CITY, ZIP_CODE.     (PROVINCE or STORE_ID alone is not enough.)

Which filter drives the search (first match wins)
  1. CITY and/or PROVINCE present  : name/address search (with FIRST_NAME, LAST_NAME, EMAIL_ID, CUSTOMER_ID).
  2. PHONE_NUMBER                  : exact match on the stored phone number.
  3. EMAIL_ID                      : "starts with" match on the stored e-mail address.
  4. SYSTEM_REFERENCE_ID           : exact match on the identifier of type SYSTEM_REFERENCE.
  5. otherwise                     : search on CUSTOMER_ID / FIRST_NAME / LAST_NAME.
  ZIP_CODE (5 to 9 characters, optionally with ZIP4 and names) adds postal-code matches and
  switches off the name search in 1 and 5.
  CUSTOMER_ID and SYSTEM_REFERENCE then act as filters on whatever was found: if CUSTOMER_ID is
  sent, only that customer can be returned.
  Disabled customers are never returned. No match at all returns E105.
    </xs:documentation>
  </xs:annotation>

  <xs:element name="REQUEST">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Request envelope. The service does not check the root element name; REQUEST is the convention.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:sequence>
        <xs:element ref="CRITERIA"/>
      </xs:sequence>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>

  <xs:element name="CRITERIA">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Search filters. All children are optional and may appear in any order.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:all>
        <xs:element name="REQUEST_DATE" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Caller timestamp. Accepted but not used by the service.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="CUSTOMER_ID" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Customer identifier (e.g. 1234567). When sent, the result is restricted to this customer.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="SYSTEM_REFERENCE" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Type of the external identifier in SYSTEM_REFERENCE_ID. Open value set
(identifier types are configured per environment). Values with documented behaviour:
  LOYALTY_ID  - if the identifier is not found as sent it is retried left-padded with zeros to 8 digits,
                then with leading zeros removed.
  POS_ID, APTOS_ID, DW_ID, ATG_CUST_ID, CHEETAHMAIL_ID, LEGACY_ID, WINDSS_ID, AFFINITY_ID,
  HARTEHANKS_ID, HH_HOUSEHOLD_ID - looked up by exact value.
Sent without SYSTEM_REFERENCE_ID it filters the result to customers that have an identifier of this type.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="SYSTEM_REFERENCE_ID" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Identifier value. Leading "W"/"w" characters are removed before the lookup.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="EMAIL_ID" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>E-mail address, matched as a prefix ("starts with"); surrounding whitespace is trimmed. Not format-checked.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="PHONE_NUMBER" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Phone number, matched exactly against the stored number: send digits only, area code included (e.g. 5555550100).</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="FIRST_NAME" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>First name filter.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="LAST_NAME" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Last name filter.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="CITY" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>City filter.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="PROVINCE" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>State / province code filter (e.g. XX). This is the element the service uses for state filtering.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="STATE" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Recognised but IGNORED by the service. Use PROVINCE to filter by state/province.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="ZIP_CODE" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Postal code filter. Only lengths 5 to 9 are searched: the first 5 characters match the
main postal code, any further characters match the start of the extension (all 9 = exact). Other lengths match nothing.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="ZIP4" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Postal code extension, matched exactly together with the first 5 characters of ZIP_CODE.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="STORE_ID" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Store context (e.g. 001), used for brand-level solicitation flags. Defaults to the
first store configured in the environment when omitted. An unknown store returns E127.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="RECORDS_LIMIT" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
          <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>Maximum customers to return; integer, default 10 (a non-numeric value falls back to 10).
The limit is only applied when more than 11 customers matched, so up to 11 can be returned regardless.
Keep the value between 1 and 10: a limit larger than the number of matches, when more than 11 matched,
makes the request fail with an unhandled error instead of a result.</xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
        </xs:element>
      </xs:all>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>
</xs:schema>
